{#- Symposed Publication Guide. House style: plain English for beginners, no
    semicolons, no dashes used as punctuation. Step names come from m.steps
    only (see guide_model.steps) so every reference matches. -#}
{%- set S = {} -%}
{%- for st in m.steps %}{% set _ = S.update({st.key: st}) %}{% endfor -%}
{%- macro sref(key) %}<strong>step {{ S[key].number }} ({{ S[key].name }})</strong>{% endmacro -%}
{%- macro banner(key) -%}
{%- set st = S[key] -%}
<div class="banner" id="step-{{ st.number }}" style="background: {{ colour(st.number) }}">
  <div class="num">{{ st.number }}</div>
  <div>
    <div class="label">Step {{ st.number }} of {{ m.steps|length }}</div>
    <h2 data-n="{{ st.number }}">{{ st.name }}</h2>
    <div class="sum">{{ st.summary }}</div>
  </div>
</div>
{%- endmacro -%}
{%- macro edit_box(what) -%}
<div class="box box-edit"><div class="box-title">This is a starting point</div>
<p>{{ what }} Change it as you see fit. You know your field, your supervisor and your
team, and you will learn more about the literature as you go.</p></div>
{%- endmacro -%}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en-GB">
<head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>{{ m.title }}</title>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="guide.css"></head>
<body>

{# ===================================================================== cover #}
<section class="cover">
  <div class="brand"><img src="logo-cream.svg" alt=""><span>Symposed</span></div>
  <div class="kicker">Publication Guide · {{ m.publication_type|capitalize }}</div>
  <h1 class="guidetitle">{{ m.title }}</h1>
  <span class="pill">Your step by step plan from question to journal</span>
  <div class="for">Prepared for {{ m.prepared_for or "you" }}</div>
  <div class="built">Built around your answers: {{ profile_line }}</div>
  <div class="dates">
    <div><b>{{ fmt_date(m.generated_on) }}</b>Guide prepared</div>
    <div><b>{{ fmt_date(m.search_date) }}</b>Searches run</div>
    <div><b>{{ m.novelty_date }}</b>Question checked for novelty</div>
  </div>
</section>

{# ================================================================== contents #}
<section>
  <h1 class="section-title" style="string-set: section 'Contents'">Contents</h1>
  <ul class="toc">
    <li><a href="#read-first">Read this first</a></li>
    <li><a href="#glance">Your project at a glance</a></li>
    <li><a href="#how-to-use">How to use this guide</a></li>
    {%- for st in m.steps %}
    <li><span class="tn" style="background: {{ colour(st.number) }}">{{ st.number }}</span><a href="#step-{{ st.number }}">{{ st.name }}</a></li>
    {%- endfor %}
    <li><a href="#glossary">Appendix A. Glossary of terms</a></li>
    <li><a href="#search-log">Appendix B. Every search we ran</a></li>
    {%- if m.references %}<li><a href="#references">Appendix C. References</a></li>{% endif %}
  </ul>
</section>

{# ============================================================ read this first #}
<section class="section" id="read-first">
  <h1 class="section-title">Read this first</h1>
  <p class="lead">Please read these notes before you start. They explain what this guide is,
  what it is not, and what you are responsible for.</p>

  <div class="box box-warn"><div class="box-title">This guide is a set of recommendations</div>
  <p>Everything in this guide is our recommendation, not a rule you have to follow to the
  letter. You are the author of your research. Symposed cannot take responsibility for any
  research you produce, for the decisions you make while doing it, or for whether it is
  published.</p></div>

  <div class="box box-warn"><div class="box-title">Everything here is a starting point</div>
  <p>The search strategies, the list of suggested papers, the protocol, the decision trees and
  the PROSPERO draft are all drafts. At every step, edit them as you see fit. They are there
  to save you time, not to replace your own judgement.</p></div>

  <div class="box box-warn"><div class="box-title">Screen the papers yourself</div>
  <p>We strongly recommend that you do your own screening of every paper. In
  {{ sref("screen") }} we list papers we think are worth reading for your project, but we
  could not read the full text of most of them, and we do not tell you which to include. That
  decision is yours.</p></div>

  <div class="box box-warn"><div class="box-title">Novelty is never permanent</div>
  <p>We checked your question against PROSPERO and the published literature on
  {{ m.novelty_date }}. New reviews are registered and published every day, so someone could
  register or publish the same question before you. It is your responsibility to check that
  your question is still new before you start and again before you submit. The sooner you
  {% if m.systematic %}register your protocol{% else %}start{% endif %}, the smaller this
  risk.</p></div>

  <div class="box box-warn"><div class="box-title">Our standard disclaimers</div>
  {%- for para in disclaimers %}<p>{{ para }}</p>{% endfor %}</div>

  <div class="box box-tip"><div class="box-title">Get a supervisor on board</div>
  <p>You do not need a supervisor to start, and this guide is written so you can begin on your
  own. We do strongly recommend showing your question and plan to a supervisor early. They can
  confirm the idea is sound and new in their field, spot problems before you spend weeks on
  them, and help you submit to a journal. Supervisors are far more likely to say yes when you
  bring a defined project that is already under way, as you will with this guide.</p></div>
</section>

{# =========================================================== project at glance #}
<section class="section" id="glance">
  <h1 class="section-title">Your project at a glance</h1>
  <p class="lead">Your research question, why it matters, how big the job is, and the nine
  steps from here to a journal.</p>

  <div class="box box-quote">{{ m.title }}</div>

  <h3>Your question broken down (PICO)</h3>
  <p>PICO splits a question into the <strong>P</strong>opulation, the
  <strong>I</strong>ntervention or exposure, the <strong>C</strong>omparator and the
  <strong>O</strong>utcomes. It keeps your search and your decisions focused.</p>
  <table class="keyval claret"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>For your question</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  {%- for k, v in m.pico %}<tr><td>{{ k }}</td><td>{{ v }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
  </tbody></table>

  <h3>Why this question matters</h3>
  <p>{{ m.rationale }}</p>

  <h3>How big is the job?</h3>
  <div class="stats">
    <div class="stat"><b>{{ m.papers|length }}</b><span>papers we suggest you read (step 4)</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>{{ "{:,}".format(m.feasibility.records_to_screen or 0) }}</b><span>records you can expect to screen in PubMed alone</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>{{ m.profile.team }}</b><span>people we recommend, so two screen every paper</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>{{ m.timeline_assumptions.total_weeks }}</b><span>weeks at {{ "%g"|format(m.timeline_assumptions.hours_per_week_each) }} hours a week each</span></div>
  </div>

  <h3>Your nine steps</h3>
  <div class="flow">
  {%- for st in m.steps %}
    <div class="fs" style="background: {{ colour(st.number) }}"><b>{{ st.number }}</b><span class="t">{{ st.name }}</span><div class="d">{{ st.summary }}</div></div>
  {%- endfor %}
  </div>

  <h3>Your timeline</h3>
  <div class="figure">{{ gantt_svg|safe }}</div>
  <p class="caption">Based on {{ "%g"|format(m.timeline_assumptions.hours_per_week_each) }} hours a
  week for each person, a team of {{ m.timeline_assumptions.team }}, about
  {{ "{:,}".format(m.timeline_assumptions.records_to_screen or 0) }} records to screen and about
  {{ m.timeline_assumptions.eligible_estimate }} papers to read in full. Steps that share a week
  overlap. If you have more time, it will go faster.</p>
</section>

{# ================================================================ how to use #}
<section class="section" id="how-to-use">
  <h1 class="section-title">How to use this guide</h1>
  <p class="lead">Work through the steps in order. Each step starts with a coloured banner, tells
  you what to do in numbered instructions and links to every free tool you need.</p>
  <ol class="howto">
    <li>Read <strong>Read this first</strong> and <strong>Your project at a glance</strong>.</li>
    <li>Share this guide with your team, and with a supervisor if you have one.</li>
    <li>Do each step in order and tick the checklist at the end of it before moving on.</li>
    <li>Whenever you meet a word you do not know, look it up in the
      <a href="#glossary">glossary</a> at the back.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Files that come with this guide</h3>
  <div class="files">
    <div class="file"><b>Paper tracker (Excel)</b>Every suggested paper, ready for screening,
    data extraction and risk of bias, with drop down choices.</div>
    <div class="file"><b>Protocol (Word)</b>Your plan for the review, ready for you to edit.</div>
    {%- if m.systematic %}
    <div class="file"><b>PROSPERO draft (Word)</b>The registration form with the fields filled
    in for you.</div>
    {%- endif %}
  </div>
  <h3>Free tools you will use</h3>
  <table class="teal"><thead><tr><th style="width:22%">Tool</th><th>What it is for</th><th style="width:30%">Link</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>PubMed</td><td>Searching for medical research. Free for everyone.</td><td><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/">pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov</a></td></tr>
    {%- if m.systematic %}<tr><td>PROSPERO</td><td>Checking your question is new and registering your protocol.</td><td><a href="https://www.crd.york.ac.uk/prospero/">crd.york.ac.uk/prospero</a></td></tr>{% endif %}
    <tr><td>Rayyan</td><td>Screening papers with your team. Free and easy to use.</td><td><a href="https://www.rayyan.ai/">rayyan.ai</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Zotero</td><td>A free reference manager that stores your papers and formats your references.</td><td><a href="https://www.zotero.org/">zotero.org</a></td></tr>
    {%- for t in m.rob_tools %}<tr><td>{{ t.name }}</td><td>Judging {{ t.use_for }}.</td><td><a href="{{ t.link }}">{{ short_url(t.link) }}</a></td></tr>{% endfor %}
    <tr><td>Excel or Google Sheets</td><td>Opening the paper tracker that comes with this guide.</td><td><a href="https://sheets.google.com/">sheets.google.com</a></td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
</section>

{# ============================================================ step: check #}
<section class="section">
  {{ banner("check") }}
  <p>Before you put in any work, make sure nobody has already answered your question. We did
  this check for you, and this is what we found:</p>
  <div class="box box-note"><div class="box-title">Our novelty check</div><p>{{ novelty_statement }}</p></div>

  <h3>Check it again yourself</h3>
  <ol class="howto">
    {%- if m.systematic %}
    <li>Go to <a href="https://www.crd.york.ac.uk/prospero/">PROSPERO</a> and search for the
    main words in your title. Read the titles of any recent registrations.</li>
    {%- endif %}
    <li>Open <a href="{{ review_check_url }}">this PubMed search for existing reviews</a> on
    your topic. It uses search strategy {{ m.papers_search_number or 1 }} with the systematic
    review filter switched on. Look at anything published since {{ fmt_date(m.search_date) }}.</li>
    <li>If something looks very close to your question, read its abstract. If it answers the
    same question in the same people, contact us before you start and we will help you adjust
    the question.</li>
    <li>Show your question to a supervisor or senior colleague in the field and ask whether they
    know of any similar work in progress.</li>
  </ol>

  {%- if m.similar %}
  <h3>The closest existing work, and how yours differs</h3>
  <table class="slate"><thead><tr><th style="width:45%">Existing review or registration</th><th>How your question is different</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  {%- for s in m.similar %}<tr><td><a href="{{ s.url }}">{{ s.title }}</a></td><td>{{ s.difference or "Read this one and decide whether it overlaps with your question." }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
  </tbody></table>
  {%- endif %}

  <h3>Bringing in a supervisor</h3>
  <p>A supervisor is usually a senior doctor, dentist or academic in the field. You do not need
  one to start, but it is a good idea to have one before you submit. To ask someone:</p>
  <ol class="howto">
    <li>Choose someone who works or publishes in this area. Your own department is a good place
      to start.</li>
    <li>Send a short email with your question, one paragraph on why it matters and the plan in
      this guide. Say how far you have already got.</li>
    <li>Ask them to confirm the idea is sound and new, and whether they would be willing to
      check your work and help you submit.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="keep"><h4>Before you move on</h4>
  <ul class="check"><li>You searched{% if m.systematic %} PROSPERO and{% endif %} PubMed yourself and found nothing that answers your question.</li>
  <li>You are happy with the question, or you have edited it.</li></ul></div>
</section>

{# ========================================================= step: protocol #}
<section class="section">
  {{ banner("protocol") }}
  <p>A protocol is your written plan for the review, made before you start searching. It sets
  out the question, which papers count and how you will analyse them, so that you and your
  team make decisions the same way and nobody can say you changed the rules to suit the
  results. Some people call it a standard operating procedure or SOP.</p>
  {{ edit_box("We have drafted your protocol in the Word file that comes with this guide.") }}

  <h3>Aim</h3><p>{{ m.aims }}</p>
  {%- if m.objectives %}<h3>Objectives</h3><ul>{% for o in m.objectives %}<li>{{ o }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>{% endif %}

  <h3>Which papers count (eligibility criteria)</h3>
  <table class="teal"><thead><tr><th style="width:25%">Rule</th><th>What a paper needs</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>Population</strong></td><td>{{ m.criteria.population }}</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Intervention or exposure</strong></td><td>{{ m.criteria.intervention }}</td></tr>
    {%- if m.criteria.comparator %}<tr><td><strong>Comparator</strong></td><td>{{ m.criteria.comparator }}</td></tr>{% endif %}
    <tr><td><strong>Outcomes</strong></td><td>{{ m.criteria.outcomes }}</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Study designs</strong></td><td>{{ m.criteria.designs }}</td></tr>
    {%- if m.criteria.setting %}<tr><td><strong>Setting</strong></td><td>{{ m.criteria.setting }}</td></tr>{% endif %}
    {%- for x in m.criteria.other_inclusion %}<tr><td><strong>Also needed</strong></td><td>{{ x }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
  </tbody></table>
  {%- if m.criteria.exclusion %}
  <table class="claret"><thead><tr><th>Leave a paper out if</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  {%- for x in m.criteria.exclusion %}<tr><td>{{ x }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table>
  {%- endif %}
  {%- if m.criteria_reasoning %}
  <h3>Why we chose these rules</h3>
  <table><thead><tr><th style="width:25%">Rule</th><th>Our reasoning</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  {%- for r in m.criteria_reasoning %}<tr><td><strong>{{ r.criterion }}</strong></td><td>{{ r.reasoning }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
  </tbody></table>
  {%- endif %}

  {%- if m.systematic %}
  <h3>Register your protocol on PROSPERO</h3>
  {%- for para in registration_urgency %}<p>{{ para }}</p>{% endfor %}
  <ol class="howto">
    <li>Edit the protocol with your team until you are all happy with it.</li>
    <li>Create a free account on <a href="https://www.crd.york.ac.uk/prospero/">PROSPERO</a> and
      start a new registration.</li>
    <li>Copy each field from the PROSPERO draft Word file into the form. Fill in the parts only
      you can, such as the names of your team, the dates and any funding.</li>
    <li>Submit it before you start screening. Write your registration number (it starts with
      CRD) on your protocol and in your paper.</li>
  </ol>
  {{ edit_box("The PROSPERO draft is written from the protocol above.") }}
  {%- else %}
  <div class="box box-tip"><div class="box-title">Registration is optional for this review type</div>
  <p>PROSPERO only accepts systematic reviews. You can still make a public, dated record of your
  plan by uploading the protocol to the free <a href="https://osf.io/registries">Open Science
  Framework</a>.</p></div>
  {%- endif %}
  <div class="keep"><h4>Before you move on</h4>
  <ul class="check"><li>Your team has read and agreed the protocol.</li>
  {%- if m.systematic %}<li>You have submitted your PROSPERO registration.</li>{% endif %}</ul></div>
</section>

{# =========================================================== step: search #}
<section class="section">
  {{ banner("search") }}
  <p>A search strategy is the exact text you type into a database to find papers. It combines
  words with <strong>OR</strong> (either word, which widens the search) and
  <strong>AND</strong> (both ideas, which narrows it). It also uses MeSH terms, which are
  PubMed's standard topic labels, and the <code>*</code> symbol, which finds every ending of a
  word.</p>
  <table class="slate compact keep"><thead><tr><th style="width:24%">You will see</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td><code>[tiab]</code></td><td>Look for the word in the title or abstract.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>[Mesh]</code></td><td>Search the MeSH topic label, which also finds papers that use other words for it.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>child*</code></td><td>Finds child, children, childhood and so on.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>"two words"</code></td><td>Finds the exact phrase.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>( ... OR ... )</code></td><td>Any of the words in the brackets.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>

  <h3>Your search strategies</h3>
  <p>We wrote {{ m.searches|length }} numbered search strategies and ran each one in PubMed on
  {{ fmt_date(m.search_date) }}. {% if m.recommended_search_number %}We recommend
  <strong>search strategy {{ m.recommended_search_number }}</strong> for your own search.{% endif %}
  {{ m.recommendation_reason }}</p>
  {{ edit_box("These strategies are drafts. As you read papers you will find new words for your topic, so add them.") }}
  {%- for s in m.searches %}
  <div class="search{% if s.recommended %} rec{% endif %}">
    <div class="search-head"><span class="sn">Search strategy {{ s.number }}</span>
      <span><strong>{{ s.name }}</strong></span>
      {% if s.recommended %}<span class="tag tag-rec">RECOMMENDED</span>{% endif %}</div>
    <div class="search-body">
      <p>{{ s.purpose }}</p>
      <span class="query">{{ s.query }}</span>
      <p class="small">PubMed records on {{ fmt_date(m.search_date) }}: <strong>{{ "{:,}".format(s.count) if s.count is not none else "not available" }}</strong>
        · <a href="{{ pubmed_url(s.query) }}">Open this search in PubMed</a></p>
      {%- if m.screenshots.get(s.number|string) %}
      <img class="shot" src="{{ m.screenshots[s.number|string] }}" alt="">
      <div class="caption">What search strategy {{ s.number }} looked like in PubMed on {{ fmt_date(m.search_date) }}. Paste the strategy into the search box exactly as written.</div>
      {%- endif %}
    </div>
  </div>
  {%- endfor %}

  <h3>How to run and save your search in PubMed</h3>
  <ol class="howto">
    <li>Go to <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/">PubMed</a>, paste the strategy into the
      search box and press Search.</li>
    <li>Write down the date and the number of results in the Search log sheet of your tracker.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Send to</strong>, choose <strong>Citation manager</strong>, set
      <strong>Selection</strong> to All results and click <strong>Create file</strong>. This
      saves a file you can upload to Rayyan in {{ sref("screen") }}.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Other databases</h3>
  <p>Searching more than one database finds papers PubMed misses. We could not run these
  ourselves, so check the counts and adjust the syntax when you run them.
  {% if m.profile.databases %}You told us you have access to: {{ m.profile.databases|join(", ") }}.{% endif %}</p>
  <div class="search"><div class="search-head"><span class="sn">Embase</span><span>through your library, <a href="https://www.embase.com/">embase.com</a></span></div>
    <div class="search-body"><span class="query">{{ m.embase }}</span></div></div>
  <div class="search"><div class="search-head"><span class="sn">Cochrane CENTRAL</span><span><a href="https://www.cochranelibrary.com/advanced-search">cochranelibrary.com</a></span></div>
    <div class="search-body"><span class="query">{{ m.cochrane }}</span></div></div>

  <h3>Grey literature</h3>
  <p>Grey literature is research that is not published in journals. Checking it shows you have
  been thorough.</p>
  <ul>{% for g in grey %}<li>{{ g }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>
  <div class="keep"><h4>Before you move on</h4>
  <ul class="check"><li>You ran your chosen strategy in every database you have access to.</li>
  <li>You recorded the date and result count of each search in the tracker.</li>
  <li>You exported the results of each search to a file.</li></ul></div>
</section>

{# =========================================================== step: screen #}
<section class="section">
  {{ banner("screen") }}
  <p>Screening means going through every paper your search found and deciding whether it meets
  your rules. You do it in two rounds: first from the title and abstract only, then by reading
  the full text of the papers that are left. Two people should screen every paper without
  seeing each other's decisions, then agree any differences.</p>

  <h3>Set up Rayyan</h3>
  <ol class="howto">
    <li>Create a free account at <a href="https://www.rayyan.ai/">rayyan.ai</a> and start a new
      review.</li>
    <li>Upload the files you exported in {{ sref("search") }}. Rayyan finds the duplicates for
      you.</li>
    <li>Invite your co-screener and switch on <strong>blind mode</strong> so you cannot see each
      other's decisions.</li>
    <li>Each of you marks every paper Include, Exclude or Maybe, using the decision tree below.</li>
    <li>Switch blind mode off, discuss every paper you disagree on and record the agreed decision
      in the tracker.</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="small muted">Covidence (<a href="https://www.covidence.org/">covidence.org</a>) does
  the same job if your university subscribes.</p>

  <h3>Include or exclude: a decision tree for your project</h3>
  {%- if tree_svg %}
  <div class="figure">{{ tree_svg|safe }}</div>
  {%- endif %}
  <div class="box box-warn"><div class="box-title">This decision tree comes from your draft protocol</div>
  <p>We built this tree from the eligibility rules in your protocol. The protocol may itself
  need editing once you see what the literature looks like and what information each paper
  actually reports. If you change the rules, change the tree too.</p></div>

  <h3>Your paper tracker</h3>
  <p>The Excel file that comes with this guide has a Screening sheet with every paper below
  already filled in. Each screener records a decision from the drop down (Include, Exclude or
  Further review needed) and, for exclusions, the reason. The reasons in the drop down come from
  the decision tree above.</p>

  <h3>Papers we suggest you read</h3>
  <p>These are the papers we found using <strong>search strategy {{ m.papers_search_number or 1 }}</strong>
  in step 3 ({{ S["search"].name }}), on {{ fmt_date(m.search_date) }}. We think each one is
  worth reading or reviewing for your project. We have not rated them and we do not tell you
  which to include. What you do with them is up to you.</p>
  <div class="box box-warn"><div class="box-title">These are suggestions, not your screening</div>
  <p>We could read the full text of only some of these papers. Please screen every paper
  yourself, and remember your own search will find papers that are not on this list.</p></div>
  {%- if m.papers %}
  <table class="compact"><thead><tr>
    <th style="width:4%">#</th><th style="width:15%">First author, year</th><th>Title</th>
    <th style="width:16%">Journal</th><th style="width:15%">Likely publication type</th>
    <th style="width:10%">Sample size</th><th style="width:11%">Link</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  {%- for p in m.papers %}<tr>
    <td>{{ p.n }}</td><td>{{ p.first_author }}{% if p.first_published or p.year %}, {{ p.first_published or p.year }}{% endif %}</td>
    <td>{{ p.title }}</td><td>{{ p.journal }}</td>
    <td>{{ p.publication_type }}<br><span class="tag {{ 'tag-pubmed' if p.publication_type_basis == 'PubMed' else 'tag-guess' }}">{{ 'from PubMed' if p.publication_type_basis == 'PubMed' else ('our estimate' if p.publication_type_basis == 'our estimate' else p.publication_type_basis) }}</span></td>
    <td>{{ p.sample_size or "Not stated" }}</td>
    <td><a href="{{ p.url }}">{{ p.id_label }} {{ p.pmid }}</a>{% if p.full_text_open %}<br><span class="small">free full text</span>{% endif %}</td></tr>
  {%- endfor %}</tbody></table>
  <p class="caption">"From PubMed" means PubMed itself labels the paper with this type.
  "Our estimate" means PubMed gives no type, so we made an educated guess from the title and
  abstract. Check it when you read the paper.</p>
  {%- endif %}
  {%- if m.own_access %}
  <h4>Papers to get through your library</h4>
  <p>These papers are not free to read online. Ask your university or NHS library for the full
  text.</p>
  <ul class="small">{% for x in m.own_access %}<li>{{ x.title }}{% if x.doi %} · <a href="https://doi.org/{{ x.doi }}">doi.org/{{ x.doi }}</a>{% endif %}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>
  {%- endif %}
  <div class="keep"><h4>Before you move on</h4>
  <ul class="check"><li>Two people screened every title and abstract independently.</li>
  <li>You read the full text of every paper still in, and recorded a reason for each exclusion.</li>
  <li>You agreed every disagreement and wrote the final decision in the tracker.</li></ul></div>
</section>

{# ========================================================== step: extract #}
<section class="section">
  {{ banner("extract") }}
  <p>Data extraction means copying the same set of details out of every included paper into
  one table, so you can compare them side by side. Your tracker has a Data extraction sheet with
  a column for each of these:</p>
  <table class="teal compact"><thead><tr><th style="width:6%">#</th><th>Detail to extract</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  {%- for f in m.extraction_fields %}<tr><td>{{ loop.index }}</td><td>{{ f }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
  </tbody></table>
  <ol class="howto">
    <li>Both extractors try the sheet on the same two papers first, then compare and agree how
      to fill each column.</li>
    <li>Split the remaining papers. A second person checks a sample of the other's work.</li>
    <li>If a detail is missing from a paper, write "not reported" rather than leaving it blank.</li>
  </ol>
  {%- for t in m.rob_tools %}{% for c in t.companions %}{% if 'CHARMS' in c.name %}
  <div class="box box-tip"><div class="box-title">Use the CHARMS checklist</div>
  <p>Your review includes prediction model studies. The
  <a href="{{ c.link }}">CHARMS checklist</a> {{ c.why }}. Add its items to your extraction sheet.</p></div>
  {% endif %}{% endfor %}{% endfor %}
  {{ edit_box("Add or remove columns to suit what your included papers actually report.") }}
  <div class="keep"><h4>Before you move on</h4>
  <ul class="check"><li>Every included paper has a full row in the Data extraction sheet.</li></ul></div>
</section>

{# ============================================================= step: bias #}
<section class="section">
  {{ banner("bias") }}
  <p>Not every study can be trusted equally. Risk of bias is the chance that the way a study was
  designed or run has pushed its results away from the truth. You judge it with a structured
  tool made for the type of study. For your review we recommend:</p>
  {%- for t in m.rob_tools %}
  <table class="keyval slate keep"><thead><tr><th colspan="2">{{ t.name }}: {{ t.full_name }}</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Use it for</td><td>{{ t.use_for|capitalize }}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Get it free</td><td><a href="{{ t.link }}">{{ t.link }}</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Parts it checks</td><td>{{ t.domains|join(" · ") }}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Possible judgements</td><td>{{ t.judgements|join(", ") }}</td></tr>
    {%- for c in t.companions %}<tr><td>Also useful</td><td><a href="{{ c.link }}">{{ c.name }}</a>, {{ c.why }}.</td></tr>{% endfor %}
  </tbody></table>
  {%- endfor %}
  <h3>How to reach a judgement</h3>
  <div class="figure">{{ rob_svg|safe }}</div>
  <ol class="howto">
    <li>Read the tool's guidance document once, from the link above.</li>
    <li>Two people judge every included study independently, using the Risk of bias sheet in your
      tracker, then agree any differences.</li>
    <li>Use the judgements when you interpret your results. Give less weight to studies at high
      risk of bias.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="keep"><h4>Before you move on</h4>
  <ul class="check"><li>Every included study has a judgement for every part of the tool and an
  overall judgement.</li></ul></div>
</section>

{# ========================================================== step: analyse #}
<section class="section">
  {{ banner("analyse") }}
  <p>Now you bring the results of the included studies together. This is called synthesis.
  {% if m.systematic %}If the studies are similar enough you can combine their numbers in a
  meta-analysis. If not, you describe and compare them in words.{% else %}In a literature review
  you group the findings into themes and compare what the studies found.{% endif %}</p>
  <h3>Our suggested approach</h3>
  <p>{{ m.analysis }}</p>
  {%- if m.skills %}
  <h3>Skills you will need</h3>
  <ul>{% for s in m.skills %}<li>{{ s }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>
  {%- endif %}
  <div class="box box-tip"><div class="box-title">If you are new to statistics</div>
  <p>Ask a supervisor or your university's statistics support service to check your plan before
  you start. Free software such as <a href="https://www.jamovi.org/">jamovi</a> and Cochrane's
  <a href="https://revman.cochrane.org/">RevMan</a> can do most review analyses.</p></div>
  {{ edit_box("The analysis plan depends on what the included studies report.") }}
</section>

{# ============================================================ step: write #}
<section class="section">
  {{ banner("write") }}
  <p>Journals expect reviews to follow a reporting guideline, which is a checklist of what your
  paper must say. Use it as you write and submit the completed checklist with your paper.</p>
  <ul>{% for r in m.reporting %}<li>{% if guideline_link(r) %}<a href="{{ guideline_link(r) }}">{{ r }}</a>{% else %}{{ r }}{% endif %}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>
  <h3>Structure for your paper</h3>
  <table class="claret compact"><thead><tr><th style="width:24%">Section</th><th>What goes in it</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  {%- for h, subs in m.structure %}<tr><td><strong>{{ h }}</strong></td><td>{{ subs|join(" · ") }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
  </tbody></table>
  {%- if prisma_svg %}
  <h3>Your PRISMA flow diagram so far</h3>
  <p>Figure 1 of your paper will be a PRISMA flow diagram showing how many papers you found,
  removed and kept. Here it is with the numbers from our preliminary search. You will replace
  them with your own.</p>
  <div class="figure">{{ prisma_svg|safe }}</div>
  {%- endif %}
</section>

{# =========================================================== step: submit #}
<section class="section">
  {{ banner("submit") }}
  {%- if m.journals %}
  <h3>Journals to consider</h3>
  <p>These journals published the papers we suggest you read, so they are interested in this
  topic. Read each journal's author guidelines and scope before you choose.</p>
  <ul>{% for j, n in m.journals %}<li>{{ j }} ({{ n }} paper{{ "s" if n > 1 else "" }})</li>{% endfor %}</ul>
  {%- endif %}
  <h3>Common reasons this type of review is rejected</h3>
  <ul>{% for r in rejection %}<li>{{ r }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>
  <h3>Declarations</h3>
  <p>Copy this acknowledgement into your paper:</p>
  <div class="box box-quote">{{ acknowledgement }}</div>
  <div class="box box-note"><p>{{ ai_note }}</p></div>
  <h3>Find collaborators</h3>
  <p>{{ collaboration }}</p>
  <div class="keep"><h4>Before you submit</h4>
  <ul class="check"><li>You checked again that nobody has published your question.</li>
  <li>Your completed reporting checklist is ready to upload.</li>
  <li>Everyone who did the work agreed the author list and order.</li></ul></div>
</section>

{# ================================================================= appendix #}
<section class="section" id="glossary">
  <h1 class="section-title">Appendix A. Glossary of terms</h1>
  <table class="keyval compact"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  {%- for g in m.glossary %}<tr><td>{{ g.term }}</td><td>{{ g.definition }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
  </tbody></table>
</section>

<section class="section" id="search-log">
  <h1 class="section-title">Appendix B. Every search we ran</h1>
  <p>Every search we ran while choosing and checking your question, with its result count, so
  your methods can be reported in full.</p>
  <table class="compact slate"><thead><tr><th style="width:14%">Source</th><th>Search</th><th style="width:9%">Records</th><th style="width:12%">Date</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  {%- for x in m.search_appendix %}<tr><td>{{ x.source }}</td><td><code>{{ x.query }}</code></td><td>{{ "{:,}".format(x.count) if x.count is not none else "" }}</td><td>{{ fmt_date(x.run_at[:10]) }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
  </tbody></table>
</section>

{%- if m.references %}
<section class="section" id="references">
  <h1 class="section-title">Appendix C. References</h1>
  <p class="muted">The papers cited in "Why this question matters".</p>
  <ol class="small">{% for r in m.references %}<li>{{ r }}</li>{% endfor %}</ol>
</section>
{%- endif %}
</body>
</html>
